6. Route Guard (Lock the Page to /admin Only)

In App.tsx:
tsx

import AdminPage from './pages/AdminPage';
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

// inside App:
const [route, setRoute] = useState(window.location.pathname);

useEffect(() => {
  const onPop = () => setRoute(window.location.pathname);
  window.addEventListener('popstate', onPop);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('popstate', onPop);
}, []);

if (route === '/admin') {
  return <AdminPage />;
}

// ...rest of your existing App rendering

Why /admin and not a button? A hidden admin link in the UI is discoverable via DevTools / bundle inspection. A URL-only entry point isn't secret either, but combined with the three-layer server check (RLS + is_admin() inside every RPC + useProfile gate), it doesn't matter if someone finds the URL — they'll see "Access Denied" unless their auth.uid() is in the admins table.
7. The Three-Layer Admin Defense (Why This Can't Be Bypassed)
Layer	What it blocks
1. useProfile route guard	Casual visitors who guess /admin URL
2. is_admin() check inside AdminPage on mount	Users who fake the profile state via React DevTools
3. public.is_admin() inside every admin RPC + RLS policies	Anyone calling supabase.rpc('admin_credit_wallet', ...) directly from DevTools console

Layer 3 is the one that actually matters. Every admin action goes through an RPC that re-checks auth.uid() against the admins table inside Postgres. The service role key never touches the browser.
